📌 What Is Spread?
    Spread = Ask price − Bid price
    It is the broker’s compensation (in spread-based accounts).
    Some brokers offer:
        Fixed spread
        Variable spread
        Raw spread + commission model
    Higher spread = higher transaction cost.

Every trade costs you the spread before it starts


Broker shows:
  Bid (sell price): 1.08000
  Ask (buy price):  1.08003
  Spread = 3 pips

When you BUY:
  you buy at Ask (1.08003)
  your trade immediately shows -3 pips (the spread)
  price must move 3 pips in your favor just to break even

This is why:
  your SL should account for the spread
  very tight SLs are risky (spread eats into them)
  trading during low liquidity = wider spreads = higher cost



📌 When Spreads Widen|increases during:
    Major news releases
    Market open/close volatility
    Low liquidity sessions
    Session overlaps ending
    Rollover time (around 5 PM EST)

📌 Practical Trading Rules
    ✔ Trade higher timeframes when possible
    ✔ Avoid trading during major news events
        Major news times (EST):
            8:30 AM EST  ← most important (NFP, CPI, retail sales)
            10:00 AM EST ← ISM, Fed speeches
            2:00 PM EST  ← FOMC decisions

        ✔ Avoid trading 5 mins before and after high-impact news
        check economic calendar daily for exact times
        most dangerous: 8:30 AM EST (US session open news)
    ✔ Prefer high-liquidity pairs (e.g., majors) has low spread
    ✔ Check average spread before choosing a pair
    ✔ Consider commission-based raw spread accounts for scalping
    ✔ Always factor spread into stop loss and take profit placement



Spread Affects:
    1. Stop Loss Gets Hit Early / Target Not Filled
    2. Spread Impact Highly on Small [A 3-pip spread on a 10-pip target = 30% cost]
    3. Low on Large Targets [3 pips on 80 pips target = negligible]